```cpp
#include <iostream>

void processVariable(int& var) {
    std::cout << "Processing variable: " << var << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20, z = 30;

    // Using a lambda function to process each variable
    auto process = [](int& var) { processVariable(var); };
    process(x); // Process variable x
    process(y); // Process variable y
    process(z); // Process variable z

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `processVariable` function is called with each of the variables `x`, `y`, and `z` using a lambda function that captures the reference to the variable and calls `processVariable`. This approach is concise and allows you to run the same code on multiple variables in a single line.

